# ----------------------------------------------------------------- 7
H1(d, "7. Public-Data Evidence")
P(d, "We do not have firm-level data on stakes, tool reach, or adoption decisions, and we do not claim to test the model. We use public data to check that three building blocks are not contradicted: that reach declines with task complexity, that reach depends on the testing budget, and that human fault discovery saturates. Table 4 and Figure 5 summarize. Details and the code are in Online Appendix F and the replication package.")
P(d, "*Reach and task complexity.* SWE-bench Verified (Jimenez et al. 2024; the human-validated subset released by OpenAI 2024) labels each of 500 tasks with the time a human engineer would need to fix it. Combining the labels with a published run of a reasoning model on 495 of these tasks, the agent resolved 39.9 percent of the tasks labeled under 15 minutes (*n* = 193), 25.2 percent of those labeled 15 minutes to one hour (*n* = 258), 4.9 percent of the one-to-four-hour tasks (*n* = 41), and none of three tasks above four hours. Resolving an issue is not the same as finding a fault, so this is a proxy at best. The sign, however, matters for the model: reach falls with complexity, which by Section 4.4 narrows the adoption interval.")
P(d, "*Reach depends on budget.* In a public set of 80,036 agent trajectories on 3,591 task instances (Nebius), the share of instances solved at least once rises from 12.5 percent with one attempt to 27.4 percent with twelve attempts. A logarithmic NHPP fits better than an exponential one (sum of squared errors 0.00023 versus 0.00104), and 68 percent of instances are never solved in the first twelve attempts. A heterogeneous model in which a share of instances are unsolvable implies an asymptotic reach of about 0.55 with a wide interval (0.43 to 0.75) for the 70-billion-parameter open-weight model, which has the most instances, while a model without unsolvable instances implies eventual reach of 1. The two models agree within two percentage points on solve rates at one hundred attempts. We conclude that reach is identified as a function of budget, *A*(*K*), not as a limit, and that a model that treats *A* as a fixed share is a simplification.")
P(d, "*Human fault discovery saturates.* We fit the Goel–Okumoto, Musa–Okumoto, and constant-rate models to 3,333 software releases from seven public Jira trackers, with 876,957 bug reports in total, of which 43 percent could be assigned to a dated affected release. The Goel–Okumoto model has the lowest AIC in 80 percent of releases. The median discovery half-life is about 1.3 months, and the median release receives 38 percent of its observed bug reports in its first 30 days. These are field reports, however, not outcomes of structured testing, and some of the apparent saturation reflects users moving to newer versions.")
TABLE(d, "**Table 4.** Public-data checks of the model's building blocks",
      [["Building block", "Source", "Finding"],
       ["Reach falls with complexity", "SWE-bench Verified with a published o1 run, *n* = 495", "Resolution rate: 39.9%, 25.2%, 4.9%, 0% across four difficulty classes"],
       ["Reach depends on budget", "80,036 agent trajectories, 3,591 instances", "Cumulative solve rate 12.5% (1 attempt) to 27.4% (12 attempts); logarithmic fit beats exponential"],
       ["Human discovery saturates", "Seven public Jira trackers, 3,333 releases", "GO model best in 80% of releases; median half-life about 1.3 months"]],
      widths=[1.7, 2.3, 2.6], align=["l", "l", "l"],
      note="*Notes.* These checks are indicative. None identifies the model's parameters or tests its adoption predictions.")
FIG(d, FIG_DIR + "fig5_public_evidence.png", "**Figure 5.** Public-data checks. Left: agent resolution rate by human fix-time class (SWE-bench Verified). Middle: cumulative share of task instances solved by number of attempts, with exponential and logarithmic NHPP fits (Nebius trajectories). Right: distribution of the estimated discovery half-life across 3,333 releases in public Jira trackers.", width=6.5)
P(d, "We also tried a crude test of the adoption-interval prediction on a public replication package of repositories that adopted an AI coding assistant. Using the number of GitHub stars as a stand-in for stakes, adoption intensity was U-shaped, not single-peaked. We do not take this as evidence against the model. Stars measure popularity, not the cost of a fault; the control sample was not documented in a way that lets us interpret the ratios as probabilities; and the sample combines individual projects with firms. The test is uninformative, and a proper test would need a better proxy for stakes, such as the number of dependent packages or production use, and contributor-level adoption.")

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- 8
H1(d, "8. Discussion")
H2(d, "8.1 Implications")
P(d, "*For adopting firms.* The model implies that an AI testing tool's value to a firm should be assessed against the firm's own stakes. A tool that is attractive at moderate stakes may lose to human testing as stakes rise, and the cutoff is determined by reach, not by price. A procurement question that follows is to ask the vendor what share of the faults that human testers eventually find the tool can find, as a function of budget, and to compare that with the firm's loss from the faults it cannot find.")
P(d, "*For vendors.* Investment in reach is lumpy. If a vendor's cost of reach lies in the interval identified in Proposition 8, it should invest up to the human baseline and stop, because the kink makes parity the efficient stopping point. Investment beyond parity has a lower return per unit cost. This can be read as a rationale for benchmark-driven development that targets human parity, though we do not claim that firms behave this way.")
P(d, "*For policy.* Because exclusion is price-independent (Proposition 3), policies that regulate or subsidize the price of AI testing services do not reach high-stakes firms. Policies that reduce the cost of extending reach, such as shared benchmarks, public datasets of faults, or R&D support, might. Proposition 9 says such support has a threshold effect: below a minimum rate, it changes nothing for vendors whose costs are in the intermediate range. This is a feature of the model's lumpiness, and it needs empirical support before guiding policy. Finally, policies that raise the stakes of failure, for instance product liability for software, have an ambiguous effect on adoption of tools below parity (Proposition 4).")
H2(d, "8.2 Limitations")
P(d, "The model is static. It has a monopoly vendor with full information, risk-neutral firms, two technologies that are alternatives rather than complements, a quadratic cost of investing in reach, and a binary notion of reach. The firm's stakes are assumed independent of its fault pool and discovery rate, which public Jira data are at least consistent with. The welfare measure excludes the benefit to end users. Parameters are illustrative. The kink results (Propositions 7 and 8) are proved under conditions (Q) and (Q′) and for lognormal stakes; the global maximum in Proposition 8 and the value of *θ*_{low} rest on numerical computation, and we have not checked robustness to other stakes distributions or cost functions beyond the lognormal and quadratic cases. The extensions in the Online Appendix are more tentative than the main results; several rest on numerical solutions on a coarse grid.")
P(d, "A deeper issue is what *A* means empirically. We defined reach as the share of the human-reachable fault pool the tool can eventually find. Public agent benchmarks measure issue resolution at a given budget, not fault detection in a production system, and the public evidence in Section 7 is evidence about the sign of dependencies, not about the level of *A*. A study with firm-level data on stakes, tool use, and escaped faults would be required to test the model's adoption and exclusion predictions. A difference-in-differences design on the introduction of a tool is a natural approach (see the Online Appendix), but it requires data on stakes that public sources do not provide.")
P(d, "The signaling extension in the Online Appendix applies known mechanisms to a new setting, and we do not claim novelty for the mechanisms themselves.")

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- 9
H1(d, "9. Conclusion")
P(d, "We separated the speed and reach of an AI testing service and showed that the two matter in different places. Speed and price decide who adopts at moderate stakes. Reach decides what happens at the top: when reach is below the human baseline, no bounded-transfer contract keeps the highest-stakes firms in the market, and when reach reaches the baseline, the exclusion disappears. Because the vendor's profit has a kink at the baseline, its investment in reach comes in jumps and bunches at parity, and a planner would invest over a wider range of costs than the vendor does. The results are structural, and the next step is empirical: measuring reach, stakes, and adoption in firm data to see whether the interval, the exclusion, and the bunching appear where the model says they should.")

